                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "NERO is an 11 year old Xbox One console game ported to PC.  It's a somewhat cerebral, \"more art than game\" adventure/puzzle game meets walking sim as you take on the role of a masked child with a dark companion and you explore themes of loss and other maudlin affairs, which involves plodding along, clicking through some Myst-esque puzzles and feeling all artsy and cultured.  Although I'm not sure how cultured you really get from playing 11 year old Xbox games on PC. \n\nFrom a technical perspective, the game doesn't meet basic minimum requirements that most PC gamers expect as standard.\n\nBecause this is designed for consoles, it carries a number of deliberate design deficiencies.  Compromises were made to cater to the inferior console gaming appliances that the game was designed for.  These are unfortunate compromises and limitations that PC gamers shouldn't be forced to accept, but it's evident that PC was a second thought for the console-centric developers.  The game is deficient as a result of these choices, and would have been so much better without the handicaps that designing games for consoles forces upon a game.  Once more, console peasants have made gaming worse for everyone.\n\nDue to the age of this game, and the lack of modernisation, the graphics appear quite dated.  This compares poorly to more contemporary games with professional standards.  Given that gaming is primarily a visual medium, this is a bit of a problem, so should be taken into account when considering whether to get this game.\n\nThe controls can't be customised, which will be an annoyance for many, but it can also render the game unplayable for differently-abled gamers, left handed gamers or gamers using AZERTY or other international keyboard layouts.  \n\nThose technical flaws drag this game well below the minimum quality standards expected of a modern PC game.\n\nNext is pricing and value.  Is this game worth buying from among the 140,000+ games on Steam?\n\nN.E.R.O.: Nothing Ever Remains Obscure has the mildly optimistic price of around $5 USD, it's not worth it given the defects and shortcomings with the product, especially considering the sheer number of completely free, much higher quality games on Steam.\n\nFor comparison, the $5 asking price for this game could get you more recent games like \"Batman: Arkham Origins\", \"Portal 2\" or \"Borderlands: The Pre-Sequel\".  Quality, professionally made games like those are frequently on sale cheaper than this.\n\nUnfortunately Steam reviewers aren't presented with a \"Maybe\" option... if they did, that's what I'd choose.  This isn't a completely bad game, but it's also not one of the best.  As always, if you read my review and you can overlook the negatives I've called out, or they just aren't important to you, then by all means go ahead and buy this.\n",
